 27	/**
 28	 * An invoice raised against a project. It is built from line items, and can take several discounts off
 29	 * their sum before tax, each either a percentage or a flat amount (the percentages each taking their share
 30	 * of the subtotal first (a percentage capped at a maximum never taking off more than that), then the flat amounts, shared across the lines in proportion to what each
 31	 * charges; together never more than the subtotal), then add a percentage sales tax on what is left of the taxable lines only
 32	 * (tax-free lines are never taxed);
 33	 * its stored amount is always that subtotal less the discount plus the tax, plus any flat
 34	 * surcharge (such as a handling fee), which is added on last and is never taxed.
 35	 * <p>
 36	 * Every figure is worked out line by line: each line is rounded to the cent first and the rounded lines
 37	 * are then added up, and the tax is likewise worked out and rounded on each taxable line before
 38	 * being added up, so the totals are sums of rounded lines rather than a rounded sum.
 39	 * <p>
 40	 * A tax-inclusive invoice (for a client whose prices already include tax) instead works the tax
 41	 * back out of the taxable lines after the discount: they are inside the price, so its amount is
 42	 * just the subtotal less the discount (plus any surcharge), and the taxable base is what is left once they are taken out.
 43	 * <p>
 44	 * A tax-exempt invoice (for a tax-exempt client) has no taxable lines at all: it carries no tax
 45	 * whatever its lines or rate say, so its amount is the subtotal less the discount (plus any surcharge).
 46	 * <p>
 47	 * An invoice can carry a minimum charge: when its net total (everything above, worked out as usual) comes
 48	 * out under it, the minimum is billed instead, and the invoice records that the minimum was applied.
 49	 */
