28	    28	 * An invoice raised against a project. It is built from line items, and can take several discounts off
29	    29	 * their sum before tax, each either a percentage or a flat amount (the percentages each taking their share
30	    30	 * of the subtotal first (a percentage capped at a maximum never taking off more than that), then the flat amounts, shared across the lines in proportion to what each
31	    31	 * charges; together never more than the subtotal), then add a percentage sales tax on what is left of the taxable lines only
32	    32	 * (tax-free lines are never taxed);
33	    33	 * its stored amount is always that subtotal less the discount plus the tax, plus any flat
34	    34	 * surcharge (such as a handling fee), which is added on last and is never taxed.
35	    35	 * <p>
36	    36	 * Every figure is worked out line by line: each line is rounded to the cent first and the rounded lines
37	    37	 * are then added up, and the tax is likewise worked out and rounded on each taxable line before
38	    38	 * being added up, so the totals are sums of rounded lines rather than a rounded sum.
39	    39	 * <p>
40	    40	 * A tax-inclusive invoice (for a client whose prices already include tax) instead works the tax
41	    41	 * back out of the taxable lines after the discount: they are inside the price, so its amount is
42	    42	 * just the subtotal less the discount (plus any surcharge), and the taxable base is what is left once they are taken out.
43	    43	 * <p>
